  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to licenses to sell liquors at retail for consumption on certain premises",
    "basePrintNo": "S5636",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5636-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §64-a, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5636\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\nlicenses to sell liquors at retail for consumption on certain premises\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to provide one existing establishment and\none soon to be completed establishment just outside of downtown\nBinghamton, NY an exemption to obtain a liquor license.  These\nestablishments, who have the same owner, cannot currently obtain a\nlicense because they are located within 200 feet of a church.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\n§ 1 adds paragraphs (c-4), (c-5) and (c-6) to subdivision 7 of section\n64-a of the alcoholic beverage control law. These paragraphs outline\nthe metes and bounds of the establishments, which are within 200 feet\nof a church and provide an exemption to obtain a liquor license.\n\n§ 2 is the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nDue to an outdated state law, no restaurant or bar may serve liquor\nwithin 200 feet of a church or house of worship. This law has proven\nespecially difficult for Upstate communities who have a number of\nchurches in commercial districts. This has limited development of the\ngrowing restaurant and beverage industries in those communities.\n\nDowntown Binghamton has recently seen positive development in these\nareas, which has revitalized the core of the city and has drawn more\nand more interest in the community. The exemptions in this bill for\ntwo new establishments will begin to bring the development and\nexcitement of Downtown Binghamton to just over the Court Street Bridge\nto the West Side of Binghamton. This currently underdeveloped area\nacts as a gateway to Downtown Binghamton and will encourage community\nmembers to explore more of the area outside of Downtown.\n\nThe First Congregational Church has given its approval to the proposed\nestablishments to seek this exemption to serve liquor. These\nexemptions are necessary to bring the growing restaurant scene in\nDowntown Binghamton to the West Side and encourage further development\nof an underutilized gateway into the core of the City.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThe act shall take effect immediately.\n",
